export const debate = {"id":"donahue-fodor-case-against-christianity-2025","number":"66","assessmentModel":"5.6 Sol","assessmentRubric":"Slugfester Reassessment Rubric v2","title":"Miles K. Donahue vs James Fodor: The Case Against Christianity","label":"Christianity, evidence, and disagreement","date":"2026-08-18","duration":"1 hr 59 min","youtubeUrl":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jOYiPLT41wM","motion":"Do philosophical arguments, biblical beauty, religious experience, and resurrection evidence make Christianity credible, or do evil, disagreement, expert dissent, and Christian moral failure make it unlikely?","sourceNote":"Based on the complete debate transcript. Summaries are condensed for clarity, and quotations are kept brief and faithful to the source.","scoringNote":"Scores are AI-generated estimates of argumentative performance and are intended as transparent, revisable judgments rather than final verdicts.","summary":"Donahue defended Christianity cumulatively, while Fodor pressed comparative objections from divine psychology, suffering, religious confusion, informed dissent, and observable moral outcomes.","quotes":{"pro":{"text":"that is what is better explained by the resurrection","context":"Donahue argued that physical resurrection better accounts for experiences of Jesus as alive and the specifically bodily interpretation attached to them."},"con":{"text":"there's more than enough examples of people with knowledge","context":"Fodor maintained that persistent disagreement among informed people remains evidentially important even when the appropriate degree of confidence reduction is uncertain."}},"sides":{"pro":{"name":"Reflective Christianity","speaker":"Miles K. Donahue","color":"teal"},"con":{"name":"Skeptical atheism","speaker":"James Fodor","color":"coral"}},"score":{"pro":81,"con":83},"sections":[{"sectionId":"philosophical-arguments-fine-tuning-and-divine-psychology","title":"Philosophical Arguments, Fine-Tuning, and Divine Psychology","timebox":"4:58–23:52","score":{"pro":81,"con":86},"exchanges":[{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-divine-motivation-from-moral-value","time":"9:24","role":"Major direct reply","words":"A perfectly good God has principled motivation to realize morally valuable states, making conscious life and its associated goods reasonably expected under theism.","score":82,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply directly answers divine-psychology uncertainty by proposing moral value as a principled, intelligible basis for expectations concerning what a perfectly good creator might actualize. Principal limitation: Identifying one genuine creative reason does not establish comparative probability, because numerous alternative worlds may realize equal or greater goods through substantially different structures and histories. Live burden: The Christian case must explain why conscious embodied life receives appreciable probability among valuable possibilities, including principled restrictions on competing goods and relevant background conditions. Locked score: The strong and competent band reflects exceptionally direct burden contact and a coherent motivational proposal, tempered by compressed comparative warrant, implicit qualifications, and unresolved underdetermination among possible creations.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-philosophical-divine-psychology-undercut","time":"4:58","role":"Load-bearing constructive","words":"Many philosophical arguments for God depend upon uncertain assumptions about divine desires, motivations, and reasons, weakening their claimed predictive or discriminatory force.","score":82,"critique":"Strongest feature: The objection identifies a fundamental methodological dependency, explaining clearly why predictions attributed to theism require defensible accounts of divine motivation rather than unspecified theological possibility. Principal limitation: Its broad reference to numerous philosophical arguments leaves the relevant scope incompletely differentiated, while the excerpt provides limited illustration beyond the divine-psychology framework itself. Live burden: The skeptical case must demonstrate, argument by argument, that motivational uncertainty materially reduces comparative likelihood instead of merely introducing ordinary philosophical underdetermination into an otherwise coherent inference. Locked score: The strong and competent band reflects excellent central relevance, conceptual coherence, and calibrated qualification, moderated by comparatively thinner evidential development and partially unspecified applicability across the targeted arguments.","tags":[]}},{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-fine-tuning-background-laws","time":"22:03","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Conditioning upon natural and psychophysical laws narrows the comparison to universes where fine-tuning is expected under theism but unlikely under naturalism.","score":79,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply offers a technically intelligible method for narrowing the possibility space, directly addressing the concern that radically dissimilar divine creations swamp the fine-tuning comparison. Principal limitation: It does not establish why natural and psychophysical laws qualify as independent background information rather than observations whose occurrence should remain evidence requiring explanation under each hypothesis. Live burden: The Christian case must justify the evidential partition without selecting conditions because they produce a favorable likelihood ratio, and then defend the resulting conditional expectation. Locked score: The strong and competent band reflects high relevance and direct responsiveness, while the missing background-evidence bridge, limited warrant, and incomplete treatment of the skeptical alternative materially constrain the inference.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-fine-tuning-creative-possibilities","time":"10:55","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Divine goodness does not make our universe probable when theories of goodness remain disputed and theism permits vastly many valuable but dissimilar creative possibilities.","score":89,"critique":"Strongest feature: The response targets the crucial comparative inference, distinguishing an intelligible divine reason for creating life from a probability assignment favoring our universe or relevantly similar universes. Principal limitation: The swamping claim remains principally conceptual, without a developed possibility measure or sufficiently concrete comparison showing how alternative valuable creations overwhelm the proposed life-permitting category. Live burden: The skeptic must justify the comparison class and demonstrate that creative multiplicity reduces the observed universe's likelihood rather than merely reflecting uncertainty about divine freedom. Locked score: The very strong band reflects outstanding responsiveness, careful qualification, logical clarity, and charitable engagement, with remaining restraint attributable to the limited evidential specification of the alternative possibility distribution.","tags":[]}}]},{"sectionId":"scripture-religious-experience-and-reflective-warrant","title":"Scripture, Religious Experience, and Reflective Warrant","timebox":"31:07–40:11","score":{"pro":77,"con":80},"exchanges":[{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-properly-basic-experiential-warrant","time":"38:39","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Coherent and undefeated direct experience of God may ground properly basic belief, analogously to ordinary noninferential trust in the external world.","score":77,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply provides a coherent epistemological route by which direct experience can confer prima facie warrant without requiring an inferential proof accessible to every outside observer. Principal limitation: The external-world analogy remains compressed and does not answer the decisive problem that awareness of incompatible religious experiences may introduce a defeater demanding further discrimination. Live burden: The Christian case must specify reliability, coherence, and defeater conditions that preserve warranted experience while explaining why relevant rival claims do not produce epistemic parity. Locked score: The strong and competent band reflects clear recoverability, central relevance, and a plausible noninferential framework, moderated by partial responsiveness, limited comparative warrant, and inadequate engagement with the strongest disagreement-based objection.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-scriptural-beauty-baseline","time":"31:07","role":"Diagnostic challenge","words":"Scriptural beauty and cultural importance cannot establish divine inspiration without criteria distinguishing inspired revelation from impressive human literature and comparably celebrated rival scriptures.","score":81,"critique":"Strongest feature: The baseline challenge clearly explains why literary excellence and cultural influence require comparative criteria before functioning as discriminating evidence for uniquely Christian inspiration. Principal limitation: The move supplies little positive analysis of candidate literary features, their rarity, or whether a probabilistic signal might survive even without an agreed corpus of inspired controls. Live burden: The skeptic must show that plausible measures cannot separate the relevant scriptural qualities from human and rival religious achievements, while preserving appropriate openness to cumulative evidence. Locked score: The strong and competent band reflects excellent coherence, evidential grounding, charity, and central relevance, but its chronological status as a nonanswer to earlier philosophical moves limits responsiveness despite its substantive independent value.","tags":[]}},{"pro":null,"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-self-authentication-discrimination","time":"37:27","role":"Diagnostic challenge","words":"Self-authenticating Christian experience supplies no publicly reliable method for distinguishing divine revelation from incompatible experiences reported within other religious traditions.","score":80,"critique":"Strongest feature: The challenge sharply separates private experiential certainty from reliable discrimination, confronting the epistemic symmetry created when adherents of incompatible traditions report comparably self-authenticating encounters. Principal limitation: The excerpt offers limited representative evidence about rival experiences and does not examine whether experiential content, coherence, consequences, or defeater conditions could support differential assessment. Live burden: The skeptical case must establish that relevant competitors remain sufficiently symmetrical after detailed comparison, rather than presuming that every religious experience carries equivalent structure or credibility. Locked score: The strong and competent band reflects conceptual clarity, stable terminology, central relevance, and proportionate calibration, while limited empirical development and its nonresponse to earlier philosophical targets restrain the overall performance.","tags":[]}}]},{"sectionId":"resurrection-and-naturalistic-explanation","title":"Resurrection Evidence and Naturalistic Explanation","timebox":"45:50–57:43","score":{"pro":84,"con":75},"exchanges":[{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-physical-appearance-explanation","time":"56:29","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Actual physical appearances of Jesus better explain the reported phenomenology and the disciples' specifically bodily understanding than hallucination and social-memory mechanisms.","score":84,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply directly contacts the naturalistic model's decisive explanatory demand by connecting physical appearances with both alive-and-present phenomenology and specifically bodily resurrection belief. Principal limitation: The superiority claim relies upon the evidential standing and detailed character of appearance narratives without adequately defending those reports or comparing every mechanism within the rival sequence. Live burden: The Christian case must establish the relevant historical data and show that resurrection improves explanatory fit enough to offset its substantial prior and theoretical commitments. Locked score: The strong and competent band reflects exceptional responsiveness, central relevance, coherent explanation, and charitable engagement, moderated by thinner evidential warrant, implicit qualifications, and an incomplete hypothesis-level comparison.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-resurrection-naturalistic-sequence","time":"45:50","role":"Supporting reply","words":"Temporary reburial, bereavement experiences, memory reconstruction, and social reinforcement could jointly generate sincere early bodily-resurrection belief without an actual resurrection.","score":74,"critique":"Strongest feature: The naturalistic sequence combines psychological, sociological, and historical mechanisms into a coherent alternative that explains sincere belief rather than dismissing the earliest Christians as dishonest. Principal limitation: Dependencies among temporary reburial, experiences, memory development, and socialization remain compressed, leaving their joint historical probability and documentary fit insufficiently demonstrated within the selected passage. Live burden: The skeptical case must substantiate each mechanism, explain their coordination, and compare the complete sequence against resurrection rather than merely showing naturalistic possibility. Locked score: The mixed band reflects meaningful alternative-explanation work, respectable coherence, and calibrated possibility language, constrained by subsidiary burden contact, limited historical warrant, compressed causal integration, and nonresponsiveness to the legally earlier experiential-warrant move.","tags":[]}},{"pro":null,"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-resurrection-belief-expectations","time":"54:31","role":"Supporting reply","words":"An empty tomb and collective experiences could yield bodily-resurrection belief naturalistically, so Jewish expectations do not uniquely distinguish resurrection from the proposed alternative sequence.","score":75,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply takes bodily interpretation seriously and argues that the naturalistic account can predict both a perceived empty tomb and experiences understood as Jesus physically present. Principal limitation: It does not sufficiently establish the historical mechanisms producing those observations or fully compare how naturally each hypothesis generates the reported phenomenology and Jewish interpretive response. Live burden: The skeptic must show that the alternative independently predicts emptiness, collective appearance traditions, and bodily belief without tailoring separate assumptions to each disputed datum. Locked score: The strong and competent band reflects coherent reconstruction, strong charity, and disciplined comparative possibility, while modest warrant, subsidiary relevance, and nonresponsiveness to the earlier experiential argument keep the performance near the boundary.","tags":[]}}]},{"sectionId":"evil-disagreement-and-epistemic-limitation","title":"Evil, Religious Disagreement, and Epistemic Limitation","timebox":"59:56–80:38","score":{"pro":83,"con":88},"exchanges":[{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-consequential-complexity-defense","time":"67:11","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Compounding historical consequences exceed human predictive capacity, so only an omniscient perspective could reliably determine whether apparently gratuitous suffering lacks sufficient justification.","score":85,"critique":"Strongest feature: The response directly challenges the inference from apparent preventability to actual evidential improbability by explaining how small interventions could generate enormously divergent historical consequences. Principal limitation: General complexity does not itself show that every observed instance has sufficient justification, and indiscriminate skepticism could also undermine favorable likelihood judgments used elsewhere in the Christian case. Live burden: The defense must connect consequential uncertainty to particular evidential comparisons while supplying a principled boundary that preserves ordinary moral reasoning and positive theological inference. Locked score: The very strong band reflects outstanding responsiveness, explicit calibration, central burden contact, and an intelligible epistemic limitation, moderated by comparatively limited warrant and an unresolved symmetry concerning which likelihood judgments remain permissible.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-preventable-suffering-evidence","time":"59:56","role":"Load-bearing constructive","words":"Extensive apparently preventable suffering is comparatively unexpected if a perfectly good and powerful God created reality to realize morally valuable well-being.","score":87,"critique":"Strongest feature: The argument connects extensive suffering directly to the previously discussed account of divine goodness, producing a clear comparative prediction rather than relying upon emotional force alone. Principal limitation: The excerpt does not develop representative cases, quantify comparative expectations, or distinguish kinds and magnitudes of suffering that might bear differently upon proposed justifying reasons. Live burden: The skeptic must show that the observed distribution remains substantially less expected after freedom, soul-making, regular natural order, and unknown consequential goods receive appropriate consideration. Locked score: The very strong band reflects excellent relevance, logical coherence, careful conditional framing, and charitable recognition of theological dependence, with modest restraint for limited empirical specification and undeveloped likelihood comparison.","tags":[]}},{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-disagreement-inscrutability-or-outweighing","time":"78:29","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Religious disagreement may be accommodated if its Christian likelihood is inscrutable because of consequential complexity and Molinism, or if stronger cumulative evidence outweighs it.","score":81,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply explicitly addresses the skeptical likelihood inference and usefully distinguishes two possible answers: restricted inscrutability and cumulative evidential outweighing. Principal limitation: Neither route receives sufficient independent support, while broad inscrutability threatens favorable Christian likelihood arguments and outweighing cannot succeed without an articulated comparative evidential ledger. Live burden: The Christian case must explain why complexity specifically blocks negative predictions about disagreement without disabling positive predictions, then identify evidence sufficiently strong to outweigh the residual concern. Locked score: The strong and competent band reflects excellent responsiveness, central relevance, stable formulation, and appropriately modest possibility language, tempered by weaker coherence and warrant concerning selective inscrutability and largely undeveloped cumulative comparison.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-religious-contingency-confusion","time":"75:26","role":"Load-bearing constructive","words":"Geographic contingency and persistent doctrinal disagreement are more expected under naturalism than under a God intending broadly accessible revelation about belief and practice.","score":88,"critique":"Strongest feature: The argument converts geographic access and intra-Christian disagreement into comparative evidence by linking those patterns to an explicit hypothesis about benevolent divine revelatory intention. Principal limitation: It does not quantify the distribution, distinguish central from peripheral doctrines, or fully examine goods such as noncoercion, cultural development, plural searching, and human mediation. Live burden: The skeptic must show that actual contingency and confusion remain considerably less expected under a carefully specified Christian revelation model than under relevant naturalistic alternatives. Locked score: The very strong band reflects exceptional clarity, calibration, central relevance, coherence, and charitable acknowledgment of theological conditions, with limited restraint for incomplete empirical detail and underdeveloped comparison against sophisticated revelation models.","tags":[]}}]},{"sectionId":"expert-dissent-confidence-and-moral-fruit","title":"Expert Dissent, Rational Confidence, and Christian Moral Fruit","timebox":"90:34–116:54","score":{"pro":79,"con":85},"exchanges":[{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-expert-dissent-selection-effects","time":"90:34","role":"Supporting reply","words":"General philosophical opinion weakly bears upon theism because most philosophers lack specialist knowledge, while philosophy-of-religion populations may reflect substantial selection effects.","score":70,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply identifies a legitimate reference-class problem, distinguishing broad philosophical competence from detailed specialization in contemporary philosophy of religion and its technical arguments. Principal limitation: Claims about widespread unsophistication and specialist selection effects lack representative evidence, defined mechanisms, directional analysis, or estimates showing their magnitude relative to informed dissent. Live burden: The Christian case must characterize relevant expertise across adjacent disciplines and demonstrate that identified selection processes substantially distort opinion rather than merely complicating interpretation. Locked score: The mixed band reflects a coherent and relevant caution about expert aggregation, constrained by weak empirical warrant, partially unstable scope, limited charity toward interdisciplinary knowledge, and nonresponsiveness to the earlier revelation-based disagreement argument.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-informed-dissent-confidence","time":"93:55","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Persistent disagreement among knowledgeable people warrants movement away from extreme confidence, even when the precise degree of moderation and relevant expertise remain contestable.","score":89,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply carefully limits its conclusion to moderation from extreme confidence, while directly explaining why informed disagreement remains relevant beyond a narrowly defined specialist population. Principal limitation: The excerpt gestures toward distributions of opinion without developing reliable data, independence conditions, evidential weighting, or criteria for determining who qualifies as an epistemic peer. Live burden: The skeptical case must establish how much confidence adjustment follows after accounting for evidence asymmetries, expertise differences, dependency among judgments, and possible selection effects. Locked score: The very strong band reflects exceptional responsiveness, logical clarity, explicit uncertainty, central relevance, and charitable limitation of the conclusion, with remaining restraint for incomplete empirical foundations and unresolved calibration of the moderation magnitude.","tags":[]}},{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-disagreement-limits-confidence-not-belief","time":"97:07","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Expert disagreement should reduce confidence in theism, but disagreement alone neither establishes an overall probability below one-half nor independently supports atheistic belief.","score":87,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply makes an important calibrated distinction between lowering confidence and crossing the threshold into an all-things-considered judgment that atheism is more probable. Principal limitation: It does not fully address the possibility that disagreement possesses directional evidential force when persistent informed division is more expected under weak or ambiguous theistic evidence. Live burden: The Christian case must integrate disagreement with the remaining cumulative evidence and justify the residual probability rather than treating failure to establish atheism as positive support for theism. Locked score: The very strong band reflects outstanding responsiveness, precision, coherence, explicit concession, and careful limitation to disagreement alone, with its subsidiary relevance and incomplete evidential integration providing the principal constraints.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-christian-moral-transformation-test","time":"114:50","role":"Load-bearing constructive","words":"Claims of divine indwelling generate an observable expectation that Christians should display morally improved conduct relative to appropriate comparison populations across history and contemporary life.","score":81,"critique":"Strongest feature: The move converts Christian transformation doctrine into a potentially observable prediction, avoiding the unreasonable demand that believers exhibit perfect conduct or eliminate every moral failure. Principal limitation: The relevant Christian population, comparison groups, behavioral measures, historical controls, and counterfactual baseline remain unspecified, making the apparent empirical shortfall difficult to assess rigorously. Live burden: The skeptic must formulate measurable predictions that distinguish gradual, uneven, or counterfactual transformation from nontransformation while controlling for selection, culture, institutions, and reporting quality. Locked score: The strong and competent band reflects central relevance, coherent probabilistic framing, explicit qualification, and charitable doctrinal engagement, moderated by limited empirical warrant and substantial underdevelopment of the proposed observational test.","tags":[]}}]}],"overall":{"pro":{"score":81,"strengths":["Directly grounded divine motivation in moral value rather than leaving fine-tuning dependent upon an unspecified account of what God might want.","Mounted the debate's strongest resurrection reply by connecting physical appearances to reported phenomenology and specifically bodily interpretation.","Used consequential complexity to contact the core inference from apparently preventable suffering to evidential improbability under Christianity.","Conceded that informed disagreement lowers confidence while precisely resisting the stronger conclusion that disagreement alone establishes atheistic belief."],"blunders":[{"text":"The background-laws maneuver did not justify treating natural and psychophysical laws as fixed background rather than evidence requiring explanation under both hypotheses.","links":[]},{"text":"Appeals to inscrutability and cumulative outweighing remained underdeveloped and risked restricting skeptical likelihood judgments more readily than favorable Christian predictions.","links":[]},{"text":"The selection-effects response asserted widespread knowledge limitations and distortion without representative evidence about their direction or magnitude.","links":[]}]},"con":{"score":83,"strengths":["Pressed divine-psychology and creative-possibility objections at the exact comparative-likelihood point required by philosophical and fine-tuning arguments.","Turned suffering and religious contingency into explicitly conditional comparative evidence tied to divine goodness and intended revelation.","Preserved the bodily character of resurrection belief while presenting a multistage naturalistic alternative based on psychological, sociological, and historical mechanisms.","Handled informed dissent proportionately by arguing for moderation from extreme confidence rather than demanding suspension of belief or certainty of atheism.","Made Christian moral transformation answerable to observable expectations while expressly acknowledging that any empirical test would be indirect and qualified."],"blunders":[{"text":"The naturalistic resurrection sequence remained evidentially compressed, with several individually contestable mechanisms combined without a developed assessment of their joint historical probability.","links":[]},{"text":"The moral-transformation test lacked defined populations, comparison groups, behavioral measures, and controls needed to establish the claimed observational shortfall.","links":[]},{"text":"Several new-issue skeptical contributions were substantively relevant yet did not answer the legally available earlier opposing moves, limiting their responsiveness within the locked exchange.","links":[]}]}},"logicalExtension":{"pro":{"finalArgument":{"thesis":"The AI steelman is that reflective Christianity may remain credible when its philosophical, experiential, historical, and defeater-control considerations are treated as mutually constraining components of a modest cumulative case rather than independent demonstrations.","premises":["Moral value can provide a nonarbitrary but incomplete constraint upon divine motivation: embodied conscious communities realize agency, relationship, responsibility, creativity, and vulnerability-dependent virtues that form a recognizable package of interconnected goods.","Fine-tuning should be framed conditionally and transparently: natural and psychophysical laws may enter the comparison only where their background status is independently justified, while any unexplained lawful structure remains part of the total evidence.","Direct religious experience may confer prima facie first-person warrant, but awareness of incompatible experiences creates a defeater that must be answered through comparative coherence, moral consequences, communal checking, and fit with independent evidence.","Resurrection has explanatory force only if the relevant historical data are independently supported and the full miraculous hypothesis explains them more simply and coherently than the complete naturalistic sequence, not merely isolated hallucination proposals.","Suffering, religious confusion, moral failure, and informed dissent are genuine counterevidence; epistemic limitation may reduce particular likelihood judgments only when independently motivated and cannot erase these observations wholesale."],"conclusion":"Accordingly, Christianity may be held as a reasonable but revisable cumulative judgment if its positive considerations retain comparative force after the acknowledged counterevidence and no single component is asked to carry demonstrative weight."},"newArguments":[{"title":"Interlocking explanatory constraints","text":"A cumulative Christian case improves when its components constrain one another instead of merely adding weak probabilities. The moral account of divine purpose should predict the broad kind of world invoked by fine-tuning; the claimed revelation should clarify that purpose; religious experience should cohere with the revelation; and resurrection evidence should connect the historical claim to the same theological character. Failure of any linkage reduces the whole case, but successful cross-constraint is more informative than a list of unrelated considerations because fewer auxiliary assumptions are available to rescue each component separately."},{"title":"Costly corrigibility as evidence","text":"Reflective Christianity can treat institutional mechanisms of confession, repentance, public correction, and protection of dissenters as testable implications of its moral claims. The relevant prediction is not that Christians never fail, but that communities seriously governed by the tradition should become more capable of detecting, admitting, and repairing wrongdoing than comparable communities lacking those mechanisms. This reframing answers crude perfection comparisons while retaining empirical risk: persistent concealment, retaliation, or resistance to correction would count against the claimed transformative pattern rather than being insulated by appeals to imperfect believers."}]},"con":{"finalArgument":{"thesis":"The AI steelman is that skeptical atheism is supported when Christianity's positive considerations lack stable comparative predictions while suffering, religious contingency, informed disagreement, and moral outcomes form convergent evidence against its specified divine and revelatory claims.","premises":["A hypothesis gains support from an observation only relative to alternatives, so identifying a possible divine reason for a world, text, experience, or miracle does not establish that the observation is appreciably expected under Christianity.","Naturalistic resurrection proposals must be assessed as complete causal models with historically grounded rates and dependencies; their skeptical value comes from adequate explanatory competition, not from the bare logical possibility of each component.","Apparently preventable suffering and unequal access to revelation have evidential force because specified Christian commitments generate expectations about divine goodness and communication, although their weight depends upon carefully defined theological models.","Informed dissent primarily constrains confidence rather than independently proving atheism, but persistent division across relevant expertise is especially significant when the proposed evidence is claimed to be publicly accessible and cumulatively powerful.","Christian transformation claims should generate preregistered, risk-bearing predictions involving defined populations, behaviors, time horizons, and comparison groups, with safeguards against explaining every unfavorable result through auxiliary doctrines."],"conclusion":"Therefore, a skeptical conclusion is warranted to the extent that Christianity's positive evidence remains underdetermined and its specified expectations fit the observed world less well than relevant naturalistic alternatives, while confidence remains open to better evidence."},"newArguments":[{"title":"Predictive compression test","text":"Compare Christianity and naturalism by how economically each accounts for the entire evidence set after auxiliary assumptions are counted. A Christian model that separately invokes unknown divine reasons for fine-tuning, hidden goods for suffering, noncoercion for confusion, human freedom for moral failure, and mystery for dissent may fit each datum but lose predictive compression. The skeptical claim is not that auxiliary explanations are automatically false; it is that a model requiring many independently adjustable explanations risks accommodating almost any outcome and therefore yields less discriminating expectation than its verbal scope suggests."},{"title":"Revelation transmission benchmark","text":"A benevolent revealer need not eliminate freedom or cultural diversity to improve information transmission. Christianity can therefore be compared with ordinary robust communication systems: important messages are repeated through independent channels, central content survives foreseeable corruption, sincere inquirers receive accessible clarification, and consequential misunderstandings trigger correction. Geographic dependence, prolonged doctrinal fragmentation, and reliance upon disputed historical testimony become evidentially relevant when they resemble unmanaged human transmission more than a fault-tolerant revelatory process. This benchmark remains defeasible because a theological account may justify particular tradeoffs, but it makes those tradeoffs explicit and comparatively assessable."}]}},"year":2025,"topicCategory":"christian-belief-doctrine"};
